  ## Verdict

  **KEEP LIVE.** This is a well-built change: the guard rails, fictional-data discipline, generated-manifest pipeline, prerendered SEO markup, and accessibility are all genuinely solid — the sample data is scrubbed to an impressive degree (documentation-range IPs, 555 phones, `sample-place-*` ids, internally consistent cross-shot data, zero PII found across 73 inspected images), and nothing here can run at boot, in a build, or against production except one unguarded 2-line SQL file that is inert until someone points it at the wrong database. No CRITICAL or user-facing HIGH defect warrants rolling back a valuable, owner-requested feature. But don't call it done: fix item 1 (the unguarded identity seed) and item 2 (a real-world fence company shown with a suspended ad account, plus three more near-collisions) in the next commit, re-shoot the four worst screenshots (banner shots, media-3, clipped frames), and let the vitest start enforcing the coverage and caption rules it currently misses — everything else is polish that can ride the following deploy.

